align: right;\">\n      <th></th>\n      <th>raceId</th>\n      <th>driverId</th>\n      <th>constructorId</th>\n      <th>grid</th>\n      <th>positionOrder</th>\n      <th>points</th>\n      <th>year</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <th>13188</th>\n      <td>540</td>\n      <td>229</td>\n      <td>54</td>\n      <td>0</td>\n      <td>26</td>\n      <td>0.0</td>\n      <td>1978</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>13191</th>\n      <td>540</td>\n      <td>229</td>\n      <td>57</td>\n      <td>0</td>\n      <td>29</td>\n      <td>0.0</td>\n      <td>1978</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>17362</th>\n      <td>717</td>\n      <td>373</td>\n      <td>172</td>\n      <td>1</td>\n      <td>7</td>\n      <td>0.0</td>\n      <td>1964</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>24298</th>\n      <td>717</td>\n      <td>373</td>\n      <td>172</td>\n      <td>1</td>\n      <td>12</td>\n      <td>0.0</td>\n      <td>1964</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>17740</th>\n      <td>733</td>\n      <td>465</td>\n      <td>172</td>\n      <td>0</td>\n      <td>22</td>\n      <td>0.0</td>\n      <td>1962</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>17743</th>\n      <td>733</td>\n      <td>465</td>\n      <td>97</td>\n      <td>0</td>\n      <td>25</td>\n      <td>0.0</td>\n      <td>1962</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":8},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 1. Exploratory Data Analysis (before cleaning)\n\n**Data:** 14 linked CSV tables (F1 World Championship, 1950-2024). The main table is `results` (26,759 rows, one per driver per race). Raw tables are loaded into `raw` and never modified.\n\n### Findings and decisions\n\n**Missing values**\n- `time`, `milliseconds` (71.3%), `fastestLap*` (69.2%) and `rank` (68.2%) are post-race information. They are missing for retired or lapped drivers and for old seasons (fastest-lap data only exists from the 2000s). **Decision:** drop them from the model, since they would also leak the race outcome.\n- `position` is 40.9% missing (unclassified drivers). **Decision:** build the target from `positionOrder`, which is never missing.\n- In `races`, the `fp1/fp2/fp3/quali/sprint` date and time columns are 92-99% missing. **Decision:** drop them.\n\n**Qualifying coverage**\n- Qualifying data starts in 1994 and is patchy until about 2002 (coverage drops as low as 6% in some seasons). It is 100% from 2003 on. Before 1994 there is none.\n- **Decision (to be tested in feature selection):** compare training on 2003+ against falling back on `grid` for older seasons.\n\n**Era effects**\n- The average field size falls from about 39 drivers (1989) to about 20 (2010 onward), and the podium rate moves from 8-19% in old eras to about 15% in the modern era. Overall podium rate is 12.7%.\n- **Decision:** include an era feature, and use PR-AUC (not accuracy) because podiums are rare.\n\n**Data quality**\n- 176 rows are duplicates on (raceId, driverId), all in 1950-1964 and 1978. They come from shared drives, where one driver appears in two cars.\n- `grid == 0` appears in 6.1% of rows (pit-lane start or no grid slot) and has a podium rate of 0.000, against 0.135 for `grid > 0`. **Decision:** keep it as a separate signal and do not treat it as pole position.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# work on a COPY: raw stays untouched\nres = raw[\"results\"].copy()\nn0 = len(res)\n\n# duplicate rule: for each (race, driver) keep the row with the BEST finishing position\n# (tie-break: lowest resultId so the result is deterministic)\nres = (res.sort_values([\"raceId\", \"driverId\", \"positionOrder\", \"resultId\"])\n          .drop_duplicates([\"raceId\", \"driverId\"], keep=\"first\")\n          .sort_values(\"resultId\").reset_index(drop=True))\nprint(\"rows before:\", n0, \"| after:\", len(res), \"| removed:\", n0 - len(res))\nassert not res.duplicated([\"raceId\", \"driverId\"]).any()   # now one row per (race, driver)\n\n# key checks: every foreign key must exist in its parent table\nprint(\"raceId in races:              \", res.raceId.isin(raw[\"races\"].raceId).all())\nprint(\"driverId in drivers:          \", res.driverId.isin(raw[\"drivers\"].driverId).all())\nprint(\"constructorId in constructors:\", res.constructorId.isin(raw[\"constructors\"].constructorId).all())\nprint(\"statusId in status:           \", res.statusId.isin(raw[\"status\"].statusId).all())\nprint(\"PK unique (resultId):\", res.resultId.is_unique,\n      \"| races:\", raw[\"races\"].raceId.is_unique, \"| drivers:\", raw[\"drivers\"].driverId.is_unique)\n\n# target\nres[\"podium\"] = (res.positionOrder <= 3).astype(int)\nprint(\"podium rate after dedup: %.3f\" % res.podium.mean())\nprint(\"podium rows before/after:\", (raw[\"results\"].positionOrder <= 3).sum(), res.podium.sum())","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-03T15:52:54.482873Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-03T15:52:54.483214Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-03T15:52:54.524869Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-03T15:52:54.483186Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-03T15:52:54.523993Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"rows before: 26759 | after: 26668 | removed: 91\nraceId in races:               True\ndriverId in drivers:           True\nconstructorId in constructors: True\nstatusId in status:            True\nPK unique (resultId): True | races: True | drivers: True\npodium rate after dedup: 0.127\npodium rows before/after: 3397 3395\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":9},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 2. Data Cleaning\n\n**Principles:** raw tables stay immutable (`raw`). All cleaning is applied to copies (`res`).\n\n**Duplicate rule:** for each (raceId, driverId) we keep the row with the best `positionOrder` (tie-break: lowest `resultId`, so the result is reproducible).\n*Why:* duplicates come from shared drives, where one driver appears in two cars. His best finish in that race is the outcome we want to predict.\n\n**Result:** 26,759 rows -> 26,668 rows (91 removed). Podium rows go from 3,397 to 3,395, so only 2 podium rows are affected. One row per (raceId, driverId) is now guaranteed by an assertion.\n\n**Key checks:** `resultId`, `raceId` and `driverId` are unique primary keys, and every foreign key (`raceId`, `driverId`, `constructorId`, `statusId`) exists in its parent table (referential integrity holds).\n\n**Target:** `podium = 1` if `positionOrder <= 3`, else 0 (podium rate 12.7%).","metadata":{}}]}